# Comparison Report

Estimated noise: sigma = 0.0287, expected ||noise|| = 0.4280.

| Method | Parameter (selection rule) | ||Kx - y|| | ||x|| | fit vs noise level |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| tikhonov | 0.0201292 (lambda_GCV) | 0.4051 | 5.5444 | |dev from noise norm| = 5.3% |
| tsvd | 26 (k_GCV) | 0.4053 | 6.7976 | |dev from noise norm| = 5.3% |
| cgls | 11 (iterations_discrepancy) | 0.4254 | 4.2672 | |dev from noise norm| = 0.6% |

## Observations

- **Tikhonov (GCV lambda = 2.0129e-02)**: residual norm 0.4051
  (5.3% from the expected noise norm) -- the fit
  saturates the noise, as a well-tuned regularized solution should.
  Two independent data-driven selectors agree: the L-curve corner gives
  lambda = 2.5548e-02 (factor 1.27 from GCV);
  the corresponding solutions differ only by relative
  0.146.
- **TSVD (GCV k = 26)**: residual norm 0.4053. The GCV curve has a
  flat valley with k in [22,35] (within 5% of the minimum), and
  the valley lies below the Picard effective rank (~40), so the
  truncation is DPC-consistent.
- **CGLS (discrepancy stopping at iteration 11)**: residual norm
  0.4254 <= threshold 0.4280, i.e. the closest match to the
  noise level. However: (a) the stop rests entirely on the single noisy
  sigma-hat estimate; (b) ||x|| keeps growing monotonically after the stop
  (4.27 at k=11 -> 20.76 at k=50 -> 73.35 at k=100), so there is
  no independent semi-convergence signal; (c) the NCP whiteness check does
  NOT corroborate the stop -- its deviation curve rises from iteration 1
  (0.107) to a plateau (~0.33), so the "whitest residual" occurs
  spuriously at iteration 1 while the residual norm there (0.8627) is
  still twice the noise level. NCP is therefore reported but not used for
  selection.

## Where the methods disagree
Pairwise relative solution differences:
- tikhonov vs tsvd: 0.4943
- tikhonov vs cgls: 0.4947
- tsvd vs cgls: 0.6391

These look large, but projecting the differences onto the right-singular
basis shows exactly where they live:

| Difference | energy share in spectral bands 0-10 / 10-20 / 20-26 / 26-40 / 40+ |
|---|---|
| tikhonov vs tsvd | 0.0% / 1.2% / 58.2% / 38.1% / 2.5% |
| tikhonov vs cgls | 0.2% / 5.9% / 64.7% / 27.2% / 2.0% |
| tsvd vs cgls | 0.1% / 3.8% / 95.8% / 0.4% / 0.0% |

All methods agree on the well-determined subspace (components 0-10 carry
<3% of any difference). Essentially **all** disagreement is concentrated in
the transition band i ~ 20-40, exactly where the Picard ratio crosses 1
(signal and noise comparable) -- an inherent ambiguity of the data, not a
method defect. Beyond i=40 every method correctly suppresses the noise floor.

## Selection
**Recommended: `tikhonov`** (parameter 0.0201292 via lambda_GCV).

Rationale:
1. Its residual norm (0.4051) sits inside the noise band (expected
   noise norm 0.4280, deviation 5.3%, well within the 5-10% spread of
   the sigma estimator itself) -- it fits the data exactly to the level
   justified by the noise without fitting the noise floor.
2. Its parameter choice is the best-corroborated one: GCV and the L-curve
   corner independently select nearly the same lambda, and the resulting
   spectral cutoff (s_i ~ lambda at i ~ 26) coincides with the cutoff chosen
   independently by TSVD-GCV (k = 26, GCV valley [22,35]) and is
   consistent with the Picard effective rank (~40).
3. Tikhonov is robust on geometrically decaying spectra, and the identity
   penalty suits the smooth solution class implied by the smooth spectrum
   of K.

CGLS fits the noise floor marginally better, but its stopping depends on one
noisy scalar estimate and lacks independent corroboration (failed NCP check),
so it is kept as a cross-check rather than the primary answer.
